But here let's just keep it simple and just talk about

00:18:38.015 --> 00:18:39.140
the Bitcoin blockchain.

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.060
Okay. So, that's what the blocks are.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.130
Now you notice that all these transactions here,

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:46.540
so every block has a number of transactions typically there are

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:50.945
between several hundred to several thousand transactions in a particular block.

00:18:50.945 --> 00:18:53.500
So let's talk about what these transactions are.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:55.860
And a transaction again just to

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.385
give you a sense of how they work.

00:18:57.385 --> 00:18:59.835
I'm not gonna talk about the specifics of the transaction

00:18:59.835 --> 00:19:02.480
format, just to give you a sense of what they are,

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:05.865
essentially every transaction corresponds to

00:19:05.865 --> 00:19:08.815
a transfer of

00:19:08.815 --> 00:19:11.010
potentially a transfer of funds between

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.215
addresses in the Bitcoin network.

00:19:13.215 --> 00:19:17.365
Yeah. So, what happens is in a transaction we have these inputs to the transaction.

00:19:17.365 --> 00:19:19.350
These are kind of

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:22.650
the addresses that provide funds into the transaction.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:25.540
So in this case there are two transaction inputs and

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:30.160
then the transaction output which are basically where the funds are going.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:32.640
So, every transaction output basically has

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.390
a value which is how much money is being transferred

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:37.530
to this particular address and then it has

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:40.090
the address which is where the funds are actually going.

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:43.035
All right. Transaction outputs contain value and address.

00:19:43.035 --> 00:19:45.630
And in this case there are two transaction outputs,

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.410
we call these things UTXOs.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:51.340
UTXO stands for unspent transaction outputs.

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.885
So, here we have UTXO on number one and UTXO on number two.

00:19:54.885 --> 00:19:56.460
So, what happened here,

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:59.880
is this transaction basically created two new UTXOs.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.705
These UTXOs belong to

00:20:01.705 --> 00:20:02.960
this

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.650
address number one and address number two,

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:08.330
and each one has a certain value associated with it.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.960
Now, later on, if you want to spend

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:12.535
money from a particular address,

00:20:12.535 --> 00:20:14.980
you actually don't spend money from an address,

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:17.010
you actually spend money from a UTXO.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.175
Okay. So, there are no accounts in bitcoin, no accounts.

00:20:20.175 --> 00:20:23.550
Funds are actually held by these UTXOs,

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.465
and what this means is that

00:20:26.465 --> 00:20:28.925
when you want to spend the money from a UTXO,

00:20:28.925 --> 00:20:30.840
essentially you create a new transaction.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:33.890
Here we have one transaction input going in.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:38.275
This transaction input essentially points to the UTXO that`s being spent.

00:20:38.275 --> 00:20:41.190
Okay. So, the value that's going into this transaction,

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:43.865
is the value associated with the UTXO.

00:20:43.865 --> 00:20:47.400
And then there is a signature by the owner of the address that says,

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:51.090
"Yes, I'm authorizing the spending of this UTXO."

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.435
Right? So, remember that you have

00:20:53.435 --> 00:20:55.220
a secret key that'll

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:58.010
essentially allows you to spend the UTXO

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:01.400
and only you can cause a UTXO to be spent, or rather,

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:05.520
only whoever owns the secret key that's able to create the signature

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.405
is capable of spending a particular UTXO.

00:21:08.405 --> 00:21:11.240
So, it's kind of cool in that

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:15.340
no one can take your funds away from you other than by taking your secret key.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.185
So, if you keep your secret key safe,

00:21:17.185 --> 00:21:19.990
no one can ever spend your funds.

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:23.370
And by the way, I should say that there's a whole industry forming around

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:27.890
custodial services for holding secret keys for cryptocurrencies.

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:31.820
So, if you happen to own a bunch of cryptocurrencies maybe as an investment,

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:33.400
maybe because you're an enthusiast,

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:34.760
or maybe because you want to use

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.040
the cryptocurrencies for something, the question is always

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:41.510
where do you store the corresponding secret keys that enable you to spend those funds?

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:44.105
You can store them at your home if you want,

00:21:44.105 --> 00:21:46.670
but if something happens and I don't know

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:49.600
maybe there's a burglary, maybe your home,

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:50.910
maybe something happens to your house,

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:52.215
maybe you lose the secret keys,

00:21:52.215 --> 00:21:53.430
in some way

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:55.840
those funds can then be spent on your behalf.

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:57.490
And so, there's a whole industry of

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:00.430
custodial services where you can give them the secret key,

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:01.885
and they will manage and store it

00:22:01.885 --> 00:22:03.520
for you. So, yes.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:07.020
So, keep in mind that it's probably a good idea not to store

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.345
a cryptocurrency secret keys yourself.

00:22:09.345 --> 00:22:12.180
It is probably a good idea to give it to a custodial service

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.845
and this is their business to keep those secret keys safe

00:22:14.845 --> 00:22:16.970
and make sure that they're never lost.

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:18.730
Okay. So, regardless though,

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:22.915
we have these transaction inputs that basically authorize the spending of the UTXO.

00:22:22.915 --> 00:22:24.290
And here we have the

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:26.495
two new UTXOs that are being created.

00:22:26.495 --> 00:22:29.640
So, for example, here you see a UTXO number three was created.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:33.165
And the interesting thing about the UTXO model is it's

00:22:33.165 --> 00:22:34.785
not account-based.

00:22:34.785 --> 00:22:37.650
So, funds are held in these UTXOs,

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:41.550
which means that after you spend the UTXO, that UTXO dies.

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:45.240
Okay. So, no one can spend funds from that UTXO anymore

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:46.915
and it's as if it never existed.

00:22:46.915 --> 00:22:49.395
Okay. So, this will be called a spent

00:22:49.395 --> 00:22:52.475
transaction output as opposed to an unspent transaction output

00:22:52.475 --> 00:22:55.780
and no one can ever spend funds from that UTXO again.

00:22:55.780 --> 00:22:57.735
So, that's how funds are basically

00:22:57.735 --> 00:22:59.290
are held on the block chain,

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:02.525
and that's how they're being transferred from address to address,

00:23:02.525 --> 00:23:07.000
they're held in UTXOs and a transfer means creating another UTXO.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:08.825
Okay. So, that's the UTXO model.

00:23:08.825 --> 00:23:12.055
So, that's how the bitcoin network works.

00:23:12.055 --> 00:23:13.580
It's a pretty interesting model,

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:18.105
although I have to say other block chains actually use an account model where

00:23:18.105 --> 00:23:19.855
funds are actually held in accounts,

00:23:19.855 --> 00:23:21.700
and if you wanted to see how much money you have,

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.115
you basically check how much money is in your account.

00:23:24.115 --> 00:23:27.060
In bitcoin, if you wanted to see how much money you have,

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:29.625
you have to sum up all UTXO,

00:23:29.625 --> 00:23:32.400
that all the UTXOs that belong to your address,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.760
and that will tell you how much money you have.

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:37.090
So, it's a little harder to tell what your balance is,

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:40.060
but at the same time this model is actually quite clever

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:42.590
and that makes it quite easy to

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:46.625
manage what's live on the block chain and what's not live on the block chain.

00:23:46.625 --> 00:23:48.360
Okay. So, that's the UTXO model.

