00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.520
It's actually not that hard to figure out who these addresses belong to.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:49.050
So, literally, you can tell who transferred funds to who and how much.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:51.475
Yes, so the amounts are all public.

00:24:51.475 --> 00:24:56.190
So, and you remember these transactions all go on the bitcoin block chain,

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.140
which is in replicated all over the world.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.050
It is public for anyone to see,

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.630
and it's important that everyone can see the block chain,

00:25:02.630 --> 00:25:05.525
so they can verify that all the transactions are legitimate.

00:25:05.525 --> 00:25:08.870
We'll talk about what legitimacy means in just a minute.

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:12.700
Okay. Good. So, the problem with this is that

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:14.990
the fact that all these amounts are public

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:18.895
is somewhat contradictory to business needs.

00:25:18.895 --> 00:25:21.460
Right. So, for example,

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.595
because everybody can see who the payer is,

00:25:23.595 --> 00:25:25.975
who the payee is and what the amounts were,

00:25:25.975 --> 00:25:27.815
this is problematic for business need.

00:25:27.815 --> 00:25:29.940
Right? So, for example,

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:33.125
if Stanford wanted to be my salary in cryptocurrencies,

00:25:33.125 --> 00:25:35.720
effectively, in a bitcoin cryptocurrency say,

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:38.170
effectively, everyone could see what my salary is.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:39.870
Yes. So, the salary would be public.

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:41.710
So, that's kind of, you know,

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:44.355
contrary to, what a lot of businesses need.

00:25:44.355 --> 00:25:45.640
And, even worse,

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:47.160
like for example if

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:50.030
you wanted to supply

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:54.280
manufacturer wanted to pay its supplier in cryptocurrencies,

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:55.940
or in Bitcoin in particular,

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.880
every one will be able to see how much the supply

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.050
the manufacturer pays for the good.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:03.160
So, Ford, for example,

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:06.700
would have to reveal how much it pays for tires, just as an example.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:09.800
So, again, this is kinda counter to how to business needs.

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:13.320
Generally, this information needs to be kept secret.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:14.770
And so, the question is what to do.

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:16.459
So, can we adapt

00:26:16.459 --> 00:26:19.580
these existing cryptocurrencies, so that

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:23.300
maybe it's okay to reveal who the payer and the payee is,

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:25.640
but it's not okay to reveal what the value is, right?

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:26.880
So, it's okay to say that,

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:28.280
"Stanford pays me my salary."

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:30.915
Everybody knows that I'm a Stanford professor.

00:26:30.915 --> 00:26:34.055
What's not okay is to reveal what my salary is.

00:26:34.055 --> 00:26:35.440
Just as one example.

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:37.015
Yeah. So that's our goal.

00:26:37.015 --> 00:26:40.285
So, can we kind of hide what the amounts are in the transactions

00:26:40.285 --> 00:26:42.440
and this is what's called confidential transactions.

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:45.815
Yeah. So, confidential transactions is basically

00:26:45.815 --> 00:26:47.750
the idea of instead of having

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:50.460
the amounts to be available in the clear, like you see here,

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:52.190
the amounts are actually in the clear,

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:55.070
the idea is to actually hide what the amounts

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:58.915
and yet make it possible for everyone to verify the transaction.

00:26:58.915 --> 00:27:00.325
Okay. So, what are we gonna do?

00:27:00.325 --> 00:27:03.285
Well, instead of writing the actual amounts in the clear,

00:27:03.285 --> 00:27:05.065
like we do here, we're gonna,

00:27:05.065 --> 00:27:06.970
we're gonna write something else onto the blockchain.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:08.975
Instead, we're gonna write what's called the commitments.

00:27:08.975 --> 00:27:10.560
So what is a commitment? So here.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:12.345
I have to do a little bit of math here.

00:27:12.345 --> 00:27:14.575
I cannot give a talk without doing some math.

00:27:14.575 --> 00:27:17.220
So, let me explain what this means.

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:19.125
So, if you look at the amounts here,

00:27:19.125 --> 00:27:22.010
you see it says, 0.533 Bitcoins.

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:26.185
Instead of writing the numbers 0.533 in the clear, instead,

00:27:26.185 --> 00:27:28.600
what we're gonna write is the number

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:31.930
533 in the exponent of some base.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.965
Yes, we're gonna write G to the power of 533,

00:27:34.965 --> 00:27:36.650
multiplied by some

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:40.380
sort of a random value that hides what the value is.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:42.535
Okay? So, this is what's called the commitments.

00:27:42.535 --> 00:27:45.460
Yeah. So, I'm committing to the number 533,

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:47.420
which was the amount that's being transferred,

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:49.570
but anyone who's looking at the blockchain,

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:53.465
has no idea what the number is that I committed to 533

00:27:53.465 --> 00:27:57.020
because this number has been blinded by this random number here,

00:27:57.020 --> 00:28:00.605
by this H to the R1 where R1 is a totally random number.

00:28:00.605 --> 00:28:04.515
And they do the same thing, you can see this is a transfer of 1.478.

00:28:04.515 --> 00:28:07.650
Well, I'm gonna write G to the power of 1478, right?

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:10.199
So, I'm committing to a number 1478,

00:28:10.199 --> 00:28:12.480
but nobody can tell that that's the number that I committed to,

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:14.035
and the same thing on the outputs.

00:28:14.035 --> 00:28:17.530
And one thing that's interesting is actually that

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:19.860
the fees are still gonna be- you noticed this--

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:21.140
let me kind of jump here.

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:22.760
The fees are still

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:25.205
available in the clear, so everybody can know

00:28:25.205 --> 00:28:26.840
what the miners were receiving,

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.495
but everything else is hidden.

00:28:29.495 --> 00:28:31.740
Okay. So, let me just say,

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:33.260
say a few more words about this commitment.

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.070
This commitment is what's called a Pedersen commitment.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:38.110
It's a very basic mechanism used in cryptography,

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:41.130
and when you wanna commit to a particular value V,

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:42.480
again, like I said, you

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:46.340
put V in the exponent and then you blind it with some random number,

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:47.490
H to the R. Okay?

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:49.205
So, by looking at the number,

00:28:49.205 --> 00:28:50.410
by looking at the commitment,

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:52.830
you learn nothing about V, but whoever

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:56.675
committed cannot change the value of the number V. That's the idea.

00:28:56.675 --> 00:29:00.230
Okay. So now, we get this private, privacy,

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:02.620
more privacy preserving blockchain in that

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:04.990
no one can tell what amounts were actually transferred.

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:07.140
So again, Stanford can pay my salary,

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:08.265
no one can tell,

00:29:08.265 --> 00:29:09.805
what my salary is.

00:29:09.805 --> 00:29:13.285
But now, we run into an immediate problem which is well,

00:29:13.285 --> 00:29:14.570
if since the amounts are hidden,

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:16.565
how do you validate the transactions?

00:29:16.565 --> 00:29:19.855
In particular, what does that mean to validate a transaction?

00:29:19.855 --> 00:29:24.220
What you have to check is that the sum of the inputs going into the transaction, right?

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:27.435
The sum of the funds going into this transaction,

00:29:27.435 --> 00:29:29.830
should be equal to the sum of the funds going out of

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:32.510
the transaction plus the transaction fee, right?

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:35.680
That's kind of a fundamental equality that has to be held,

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:38.965
has to be true for every single transaction.

00:29:38.965 --> 00:29:40.990
When the amounts were available in the clear,

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:42.700
everyone could just look in the blockchain and

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:44.645
verify that all the transactions are valid.

00:29:44.645 --> 00:29:46.365
If the transaction was not valid,

00:29:46.365 --> 00:29:50.630
the whole block would get rejected and thrown out of the blockchain,

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:53.210
but now that the amounts are commitments,

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:54.860
they're not actually available in the clear,

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:57.375
you can no longer verify that this property holds.

00:29:57.375 --> 00:29:59.475
So we have kind of a fundamental problem

00:29:59.475 --> 00:30:01.075
and the question is what do we do?

00:30:01.075 --> 00:30:02.990
Okay. So this is kind of

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:05.130
where the beauty of cryptography comes in.

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:07.400
And so, what we're gonna do is we're gonna use,

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:09.170
a little bit of crypto magic and

00:30:09.170 --> 00:30:12.715
the crypto magic we're gonna use is what's called a zero knowledge proof.

00:30:12.715 --> 00:30:14.100
Okay? Zero knowledge proof.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.520
So, what is a zero knowledge proof?

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:19.200
A zero knowledge proof is something that allows me to prove to you that

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:24.075
a certain fact is true without revealing anything else about that fact.

00:30:24.075 --> 00:30:28.570
So, you learn you have confidence that the fact is actually true,

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:30.245
but you have no idea,

00:30:30.245 --> 00:30:31.610
about anything else.

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:33.675
Yeah, that's kind of the magic of zero knowledge proof

00:30:33.675 --> 00:30:35.545
and let me explain what we do here.

00:30:35.545 --> 00:30:37.885
So, again, even though

00:30:37.885 --> 00:30:40.505
the public doesn't know what the amounts are,

00:30:40.505 --> 00:30:44.870
whoever creates that transaction can convince the public that this equality

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:49.460
actually holds without actually revealing what the input and output values are.

00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:51.100
So that's one thing. So you have to

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:54.395
convince the public that this equality star actually holds.

00:30:54.395 --> 00:30:56.650
It turns out there's another problem you have to prove,

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.035
which is that in fact,

00:30:58.035 --> 00:30:59.994
it's not just about the equality,

00:30:59.994 --> 00:31:04.340
the other problem you have to prove is that all the values are actually positive.

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:07.400
Yeah. So it's not just the sums are equal,

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.130
you wanna make sure that none of the output values are negative.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.375
Let me explain quickly why that's so important.

00:31:12.375 --> 00:31:14.300
Imagine I had a transaction

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:16.605
that had three inputs going in.

00:31:16.605 --> 00:31:19.600
So there's a UTXO worth three bitcoins going

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:23.205
in and then one UTXO on the output was equal to seven,

00:31:23.205 --> 00:31:26.575
and the other UTXO on the output was equal to negative four.

00:31:26.575 --> 00:31:28.235
Right? So, we had three going in,

00:31:28.235 --> 00:31:30.550
seven and negative four going out.

00:31:30.550 --> 00:31:34.475
Well, three is equal to seven plus negative four, yes?

00:31:34.475 --> 00:31:36.895
So, star is satisfied.

00:31:36.895 --> 00:31:41.145
However, now we have a UTXO that's worth seven bitcoins which

00:31:41.145 --> 00:31:44.375
anyone can then spend as if they had seven bitcoins.

00:31:44.375 --> 00:31:47.445
Yeah. So, money was all of a sudden created out of thin air

00:31:47.445 --> 00:31:48.895
and of course they're gonna ignore them.

00:31:48.895 --> 00:31:50.185
The negative four output.

00:31:50.185 --> 00:31:53.520
They're just gonna use the seven bitcoin output and sort of all of a sudden,

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:55.005
money was created out of thin air.

00:31:55.005 --> 00:31:56.260
So, in fact

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:58.760
proving that all the outputs are positive is kind of

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:01.775
crucial to prevent money from being created,

00:32:01.775 --> 00:32:03.485
and so we have to do that as well.

00:32:03.485 --> 00:32:05.870
The odd thing is that proving that

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:07.380
star holds is quite easy,

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:10.100
proving that an equality like this is actually quite easy,

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:11.520
this should just follows from

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:13.875
the rules of exponentiation.

00:32:13.875 --> 00:32:15.535
Yeah, if I have, well,

00:32:15.535 --> 00:32:17.170
let me kind of convince you that that's true.

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:21.525
If I have a commitment you can see G to the 533 and G to the 1478,

00:32:21.525 --> 00:32:23.560
if I multiply these two together,

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:26.810
I get G to the 533 plus 1478.

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:28.850
Yeah, and I can compare that to the sum,

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:31.420
to the product of these two outputs.

00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:35.930
Yeah, so in fact, checking that a sum hold is actually quite easy,

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:38.135
checking that a value is positive,

00:32:38.135 --> 00:32:40.440
that turns out to be kind of tricky.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:43.130
Why did I write 2 to the 52 minus 1 one here?

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:44.675
Well, it turns out

00:32:44.675 --> 00:32:46.315
that's the denomination of bitcoin.

00:32:46.315 --> 00:32:48.040
Yeah, so a bitcoin

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.060
can go up to basically the

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:54.555
bitcoin has 52 bits of precision for a particular amounts.

00:32:54.555 --> 00:32:57.485
So, the amounts can be specified

00:32:57.485 --> 00:32:59.680
as a 52 bit string and we have to argue that

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:02.880
that 52 bit string is a positive output.

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:04.280
Okay, that's the hard part.

00:33:04.280 --> 00:33:06.850
All right. So what do we do? So, our work basically

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:09.445
which we call bulletproofs is a very efficient,

00:33:09.445 --> 00:33:15.445
zero knowledge proof system for proving that a commitment to a value V is positive.

00:33:15.445 --> 00:33:17.245
Yeah, the value that was committed to,

00:33:17.245 --> 00:33:18.660
in fact, is positive.

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:20.385
And it builds on some earlier work.

00:33:20.385 --> 00:33:25.630
Again, beautiful, beautiful work of Bootle-Cerulli-Chaidos-Groth and Petit from 2016.

00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:28.070
We basically improved

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:30.235
that somewhat and

00:33:30.235 --> 00:33:33.195
experimented with it for this particular application.

00:33:33.195 --> 00:33:34.615
And so, what we can do,

00:33:34.615 --> 00:33:37.300
is essentially, if you have an n-bit proof.

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:38.910
So, you have a commitment to

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:42.130
an n-bit number and you wanna prove that number is positive,

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:43.980
this is what's called a range proof.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:45.785
It turns out

00:33:45.785 --> 00:33:47.440
again, I have to write a little bit of math here

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:52.340
It turns out our proof size effectively is logarithmic in the number of bits.

00:33:52.340 --> 00:33:55.080
Okay? So, even if your number is, you know,

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:58.820
a thousand bits, the proof will only contain 10-10 elements in it.

00:33:58.820 --> 00:34:00.440
Yeah. So, it's a very short proof

00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:03.070
whereas previously, the proofs were much, much,

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:06.070
much longer, they basically were linear in the

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:08.445
size of the value.

00:34:08.445 --> 00:34:11.850
Yeah. So, we kind of went from linear size proofs to logarithmic size proofs.

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:13.800
Logarithms is, of course, grow much

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.880
slower, but even more importantly,

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:17.250
if you have to do D proofs,

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:21.275
like if you have to prove multiple ranges in a given

00:34:21.275 --> 00:34:23.670
remember every block has

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:26.250
like a thousand transactions in it and so,

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.929
you have to do like a thousand of these arranged proofs in every block.

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:33.365
Before, the size of these proofs would just add up.

00:34:33.365 --> 00:34:35.350
So, if you do have to do a thousand proofs

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:37.195
the proof would be a thousand times bigger

00:34:37.195 --> 00:34:38.690
than a single proof.

00:34:38.690 --> 00:34:40.740
In our case, using bullet proofs, no.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:42.760
You just basically, if you have to do a thousand proofs,

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:46.585
all you do is you add like 10 additional elements and that's it

00:34:46.585 --> 00:34:48.750
and then nothing else changes.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:50.280
And of course,

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:51.490
there is no trusted-setup

00:34:51.490 --> 00:34:52.945
which I don't want to talk about here.

00:34:52.945 --> 00:34:54.560
Okay. So these bulletproofs are kind of

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:57.245
extremely well-suited for confidential transactions

00:34:57.245 --> 00:35:00.380
and then just to give you an example, essentially,

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:02.464
if you wanted to implement confidential transactions

00:35:02.464 --> 00:35:05.170
using the previous best zero knowledge system,

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:07.485
if you had one transaction, the

00:35:07.485 --> 00:35:11.075
the data that would have to go on the blockchain would be around four kilobytes,

00:35:11.075 --> 00:35:13.585
whereas with bulletproofs, it's only 600 bytes.

00:35:13.585 --> 00:35:15.654
So, even for a single transaction,

00:35:15.654 --> 00:35:17.935
we save a lot of space on the blockchain.

00:35:17.935 --> 00:35:20.970
if you wanted to do proofs for like two transactions,

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:23.100
before, everything would scale linearly.

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.500
For us, things scale much, much better,

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:26.740
so instead of, you know,

00:35:26.740 --> 00:35:29.330
7,000 bytes, we only need 700 bytes.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:31.390
If you wanna do 10 proofs, you know, here, before,

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:32.890
you would need 40,000 bytes,

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:35.055
we only need 900 bytes.

00:35:35.055 --> 00:35:36.780
Okay? So, again,

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:38.600
the amount of data that goes on the blockchain,

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:41.745
all these proofs have to go on a blockchain to convince the public,

00:35:41.745 --> 00:35:44.500
the amount of data using bulletproofs is much, much,

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:49.370
much shorter than what was possible before in the case of no trusted-setup

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:50.720
And as a result,

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:53.860
this makes confidential transactions much, much more practical,

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:56.260
and easier to deploy in the real world without blowing

00:35:56.260 --> 00:35:58.645
up the size of a blockchain. Good.

00:35:58.645 --> 00:36:00.410
So, as I said,

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:02.440
this is the bulletproofs, so,

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:05.390
like a very good match for confidential transactions,

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:06.750
just to be concrete.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:10.974
If you wanted to implement confidential transactions without bulletproofs,

00:36:10.974 --> 00:36:14.715
blockchain would be like 160 gigabytes, with us,

00:36:14.715 --> 00:36:16.785
it kind of drops to like 17 gigabytes,

00:36:16.785 --> 00:36:18.730
so, you know, significant savings.

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:21.035
And I want you to remember the blockchain is

00:36:21.035 --> 00:36:23.940
replicated all over the world, all over the world.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.495
Right? So, everyone would have to store 160 gigabytes,

00:36:27.495 --> 00:36:31.285
here, we are providing quite a bit of savings in the amount of storage.

00:36:31.285 --> 00:36:33.695
The other thing that bulletproofs are good for,

00:36:33.695 --> 00:36:35.125
is proving what's called

00:36:35.125 --> 00:36:36.645
solving the solvency problem.

00:36:36.645 --> 00:36:38.670
And I'll explain this is a paper that we wrote,

00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:40.765
in 2015

00:36:40.765 --> 00:36:43.520
and how to prove that an exchange solvent,

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:45.290
and I'll talk about that more in just a minute,

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:46.735
but I can tell you that,

00:36:46.735 --> 00:36:48.900
in our first results,

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:52.945
the proof of solvency that the exchange actually can support all of its

00:36:52.945 --> 00:36:54.205
obligations,

00:36:54.205 --> 00:36:55.980
that proof was 18 gigabytes,

00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:57.045
every day

00:36:57.045 --> 00:36:58.340
would have to be produced,

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:01.260
with bulletproofs, the proof goes down dramatically,

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:03.280
to something like 60 megabytes which is much,

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:05.045
much, much, easier to do

00:37:05.045 --> 00:37:07.210
in practice and it turns out

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:09.210
bulletproofs can also be used for

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:12.375
providing other anonymity mechanisms which I won't talk about here.

00:37:12.375 --> 00:37:14.560
Okay. So, let's talk about a solvency problem.

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:17.280
So, that's kind of a really fascinating area in its own right,

00:37:17.280 --> 00:37:19.880
so let me explain what the solvency problem is.

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:21.910
So, I hope many of you have heard of Mt.

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:22.990
Gox. So, Mt.

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:24.865
Gox is a bitcoin exchange,

00:37:24.865 --> 00:37:27.980
that essentially held a lot of people's funds,

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:29.910
so instead of holding the funds yourself,

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:31.340
you would give the funds to Mt.

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:34.255
Gox and they presumably would store them for you.

00:37:34.255 --> 00:37:38.425
Well, unfortunately, through a sequence of mishaps,

00:37:38.425 --> 00:37:40.980
they basically lost all the funds that were given to them.

00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:42.650
I just wanted you to understand

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:47.360
something like $450 million were lost as a result of the Mt. Gox compromise.

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:50.015
This was a big deal in the bitcoin world.

00:37:50.015 --> 00:37:53.655
It caused the crash in the prices of bitcoin.

00:37:53.655 --> 00:37:56.765
Now this is kind of a very traumatic event for

00:37:56.765 --> 00:37:58.600
cryptocurrencies,

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:00.900
for bitcoin in particular. But it turns out Mt.

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:02.600
Gox was nothing special

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:04.530
in that many other exchanges have failed to,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:06.150
basically, people gave them

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.450
their funds to hold,

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:09.750
and then the exchange basically lost those funds,

00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:11.070
and a lot of people lost,

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:13.100
their cryptocurrency savings.

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:16.110
And again, this is why you want to use custodial services

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:17.335
because this is their business.

00:38:17.335 --> 00:38:19.325
They will not lose or presumably,

00:38:19.325 --> 00:38:21.760
that they're built to not lose your keys.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:23.365
So, don't hold the keys yourself,

00:38:23.365 --> 00:38:24.775
give them to a custodial service,

00:38:24.775 --> 00:38:27.155
that's the probably the safest thing to do.

00:38:27.155 --> 00:38:29.030
Okay. So, the question is

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:30.525
what can we do about these

00:38:30.525 --> 00:38:32.105
solvency problems?

00:38:32.105 --> 00:38:33.715
The fact that exchanges,

00:38:33.715 --> 00:38:35.265
so many exchanges, have,

00:38:35.265 --> 00:38:37.645
lost the funds given to them.

00:38:37.645 --> 00:38:39.085
So, the question is what to do?

00:38:39.085 --> 00:38:42.050
And so again, we can

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:43.950
use the fact that we're dealing with

00:38:43.950 --> 00:38:46.850
digital goods to provide what's called a proof of solvency.

00:38:46.850 --> 00:38:48.825
So, what is a proof of solvency?

00:38:48.825 --> 00:38:50.520
Well, let's see.

00:38:50.520 --> 00:38:53.875
So, every exchange has a bunch of obligations to its customers.

00:38:53.875 --> 00:38:56.140
This is how much money they owe their customers.

00:38:56.140 --> 00:38:58.375
And then they have a bunch of assets that they hold.

00:38:58.375 --> 00:39:00.545
Right? So, these are basically cryptocurrencies,

00:39:00.545 --> 00:39:02.820
crypto funds that exchange owns.

00:39:02.820 --> 00:39:05.020
And the goal is to prove that the amount of

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:08.793
assets is at least as big as the amount of obligations.

00:39:08.793 --> 00:39:11.560
In fact if you wanted to support reserve

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:13.160
currencies where maybe

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:14.480
the assets are only

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:16.985
one-tenth of the obligations, you can do that too.

00:39:16.985 --> 00:39:19.095
But let's stick to the simple

00:39:19.095 --> 00:39:22.075
case where just assets are bigger than obligations.

00:39:22.075 --> 00:39:25.035
Okay. This is what solvency means.

00:39:25.035 --> 00:39:27.590
In the physical world, the way a bank proved that,

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:30.480
proved that it is solvent, is it brings in auditors once a year

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:33.450
and these auditors certify that a bank is solvent.

00:39:33.450 --> 00:39:36.635
That is not a transparent process, not transparent.

00:39:36.635 --> 00:39:40.710
Right. Nobody, in the public knows what these auditors do,

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:43.270
they just certify, but

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:44.760
there is no

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:46.185
transparency to that process.

00:39:46.185 --> 00:39:48.310
And the beauty of a cryptocurrency

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:51.310
is you can actually transparently prove to the entire world,

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:55.275
that you're solvent because everything is stored as crypto.

00:39:55.275 --> 00:39:57.530
What they would like to do though

00:39:57.530 --> 00:40:01.975
is prove that the exchange is solvent without revealing secret information,

00:40:01.975 --> 00:40:05.125
you know, internal information that's private to the exchange.

00:40:05.125 --> 00:40:09.715
Well, this again takes us to the world of zero knowledge proofs where you'd like to prove

00:40:09.715 --> 00:40:11.785
in zero knowledge that you're solvent

00:40:11.785 --> 00:40:14.635
without revealing anything about your internal business.

00:40:14.635 --> 00:40:17.610
Okay, so again, that was a beautiful problem for us

00:40:17.610 --> 00:40:19.090
that we worked on and as I said,

00:40:19.090 --> 00:40:22.142
we developed an efficiency of zero-knowledge protocol for this problem.

00:40:22.142 --> 00:40:24.430
It's kind of remarkable that literally

00:40:24.430 --> 00:40:27.570
the exchange can run this protocol every day.

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:29.830
Yeah, every day, it would produce,

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:32.045
you don't have to do this once a year like in the physical world.

00:40:32.045 --> 00:40:35.590
Every morning, it would produce a zero knowledge proof that says,

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:37.960
yes, my assets are bigger than my obligations.

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:40.160
Nothing would be revealed about its obligations

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:42.210
and nothing would be revealed about its assets

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:45.605
and yet the public now has confidence that they are solvent.

00:40:45.605 --> 00:40:47.810
Yeah, so that's what you can do with these

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:48.850
proofs of solvency,

00:40:48.850 --> 00:40:50.405
zero knowledge proofs of solvency.

00:40:50.405 --> 00:40:52.030
And as I said,

00:40:52.030 --> 00:40:54.325
bulletproofs makes this very efficient.

00:40:54.325 --> 00:40:56.260
It's quite interesting actually if Mt.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.100
Gox had run these proofs like on a daily basis,

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:01.740
their trouble would have been detected many,

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:05.175
many months before they actually had to declare that they're bankrupt.

00:41:05.175 --> 00:41:07.060
Yeah. In fact, they would have discovered

00:41:07.060 --> 00:41:09.770
themselves that they cannot run the proof of solvency,

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:12.290
and they would have realized that there's a problem much earlier

00:41:12.290 --> 00:41:14.820
on than when the problem was actually made public.

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:17.205
Yeah. So, I have to say that,

00:41:17.205 --> 00:41:19.620
bulletproofs have been adopted by a number of projects,

00:41:19.620 --> 00:41:22.390
and they're actually put in use, somehow,

00:41:22.390 --> 00:41:26.705
somehow proof of solvency have not yet been adopted by the industry.

00:41:26.705 --> 00:41:29.100
So if you know, and if you run an exchange,

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:31.620
or if you know an exchange that it's interested in

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:34.940
deploying a proof of solvency, please get in touch with us.

00:41:34.940 --> 00:41:37.070
You know, I think this is a useful thing to deploy.

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:39.580
I think once exchange does it,

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:42.615
all the other exchanges would have to kind of deploy it as well.

00:41:42.615 --> 00:41:44.225
And, you know, it would

00:41:44.225 --> 00:41:45.970
give us more faith

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:48.215
in how these exchanges run.

00:41:48.215 --> 00:41:50.420
And so generally, this is kind of a nice thing to do.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:53.190
And as I said, we can do it so, we might as well.

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:55.960
So again, if you are interested in actually deploying and using this,

00:41:55.960 --> 00:41:57.215
please get in touch with us.

00:41:57.215 --> 00:42:03.145
All the research that we do is available in the public domain to support ecosystem.

00:42:03.145 --> 00:42:06.175
And in fact we'd be happy to help you deploy this.

