I'll just summarize in 30 seconds and then stop,

00:43:43.235 --> 00:43:44.770
which is a

00:43:44.770 --> 00:43:47.275
a way to privately aggregate statistics.

00:43:47.275 --> 00:43:51.575
And again, I put up a link to this project if you wanna learn more about what it does,

00:43:51.575 --> 00:43:55.610
but maybe I can just walk through in 30 seconds and explain what this project does.

00:43:55.610 --> 00:43:57.300
It's kind of an interesting, interesting,

00:43:57.300 --> 00:43:58.780
topic in its own right.

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.760
So, I just want you to be aware that this technology exists,

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.315
and if it's applicable to you, you know, please use it,

00:44:04.315 --> 00:44:07.570
or get in touch with us, and we'd be happy to help you deploy it.

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:10.180
So, the problem is basically, how do we,

00:44:10.180 --> 00:44:13.405
how does a company learn about its users?

00:44:13.405 --> 00:44:17.080
Aggregate statistics about it's user in a privacy preserving manner.

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:18.960
Okay? So, today, suppose,

00:44:18.960 --> 00:44:20.260
let's take a simple application.

00:44:20.260 --> 00:44:21.550
Suppose you're on Twitter.

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:23.150
You wanna measure, oh sorry.

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:25.855
Suppose you're an application that measures,

00:44:25.855 --> 00:44:27.635
I don't know, people's blood pressure.

00:44:27.635 --> 00:44:29.390
And you wanna measure,

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:33.600
how does blood pressure correlate to how many minutes a day you use Twitter?

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:37.355
Yeah, so, every point here corresponds to one user, right?

00:44:37.355 --> 00:44:39.420
So, this user uses Twitter this much,

00:44:39.420 --> 00:44:41.185
and his blood pressure is this much, right?

00:44:41.185 --> 00:44:44.225
So, if you wanted to kind of build a model for how

00:44:44.225 --> 00:44:48.085
Twitter uses correlates to blood pressure- this is just a comical application,

00:44:48.085 --> 00:44:50.280
nobody actually does this, but,

00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:53.320
if you wanted to build sort of a model for what the correlation is,

00:44:53.320 --> 00:44:55.210
the way you do that today is, basically,

00:44:55.210 --> 00:44:58.575
every user sends the data to the application,

00:44:58.575 --> 00:44:59.850
to the application developer,

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:04.480
and then the application developer runs regression to figure out what the model is.

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:06.430
Yeah, that's kind of how we do things today.

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:11.280
This is highly non-private in the sense that you have to send all your data to the

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:13.150
application developer, even though

00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:16.340
all the application developer cares about is aggregate statistics.

00:45:16.340 --> 00:45:18.395
So, this is very non-private,

00:45:18.395 --> 00:45:19.780
and the question is, can we do better?

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:21.090
And it turns out we can do better.

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:25.335
Basically, what you can do is you can split your data and share, and

00:45:25.335 --> 00:45:28.270
send a share of the data to the Stress tracker,

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:31.385
which actually reveals nothing to the application developer.

00:45:31.385 --> 00:45:34.085
You can send another share of the data to, say,

00:45:34.085 --> 00:45:35.630
Google, which again, on their own,

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:37.145
they learn nothing about your data.

00:45:37.145 --> 00:45:38.590
So, this is called secret sharing,

00:45:38.590 --> 00:45:40.954
where each, where, by themselves,

00:45:40.954 --> 00:45:43.590
they absolutely learn nothing about the individual data,

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:44.940
and yet, they can actually,

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:47.420
together, they can figure out what the model is.

00:45:47.420 --> 00:45:50.165
Yeah, so, this is great. Yeah, this is great, it allows

00:45:50.165 --> 00:45:54.125
the developer to learn what the model is without learning anything about,

00:45:54.125 --> 00:45:56.940
private data, however, there is a problem.

00:45:56.940 --> 00:45:58.940
The problem is that

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:00.850
someone can come in and send you bogus data.

00:46:00.850 --> 00:46:03.800
You can see they sent a point that's way, way, way, way,

00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:05.785
way up here, and that causes the

00:46:05.785 --> 00:46:07.215
whole model to skew.

00:46:07.215 --> 00:46:12.020
That one point is so off the charts that it causes the whole model to skew.

00:46:12.020 --> 00:46:13.970
And so, what our system can make it,

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:16.310
what our system makes it possible to do is,

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:18.864
basically, you know, learn the results,

00:46:18.864 --> 00:46:23.365
do it with privacy, but also make sure that all of the submissions,

00:46:23.365 --> 00:46:25.440
actually, lie within

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:27.575
a legitimate bound, a valid bound.

00:46:27.575 --> 00:46:32.130
So, you cannot have one submission that will skew up all the results.

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:34.205
All right, so, that's what the Prio system does.

00:46:34.205 --> 00:46:36.160
If you have a need for

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:38.955
private aggregation, I guess I'm not gonna say much more about this.

